%S A395718 1,2,4,6,7,10,11,12,14,17,18,19,20,23,24,27,28,30,31,32,33,35,38,39,
%T A395718 40,44,45,46,48,49,51,52,53,54,57,58,61,62,64,65,66,69,72,73,74,75,78,
%U A395718 79,80,82,83,85,86,87,88,90,93,94,95,99,100,101,103,104,106
%N A395718 Numbers admitted by the golden exclusion sieve.
%C A395718 Let phi=(1+sqrt(5))/2, and let b(v)=1 if v is in the sequence and 0 otherwise. Set m=floor(v/phi), L=v-m-1=floor(v/phi^2), and c=Sum_{j=m+1..v-1} b(j). Then v is admitted exactly when phi*c<=L.
%C A395718 The sequence has natural density 1/phi, or equivalently a(n)/n tends to phi. Its successive gaps are 1, 2, 3, or 4, and each of these occurs. There are no runs of five admitted integers, although runs of four do occur.
%C A395718 Every Fibonacci number greater than or equal to 3 is excluded, whereas one less than every Fibonacci number greater than or equal to 2 is admitted. Every Lucas number L_k with k>=7 is also excluded, beginning with L_7=29. The characteristic sequence is Fibonacci-automatic: a minimal 9-state Moore machine reads valid padded Zeckendorf representations, while a machine that also checks validity requires 11 states.
%D A395718 J.-P. Allouche and J. Shallit, Automatic Sequences: Theory, Applications, Generalizations, Cambridge University Press, 2003.
%D A395718 J. Berstel, Fibonacci words—a survey, in The Book of L, Springer, 1986, pp. 13-27.
%H A395718 Jake Foth, <a href="/A395718/b395718.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A395718 Jake Foth, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21660281">The Golden Exclusion Sieve: Fibonacci Substitution and Zeckendorf Automata from a Self-Referential Density Rule</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A395718 Let phi=(1+sqrt(5))/2 and g(v)=floor(v/phi). Then a(n)=min{v>=1: A(v)>=n}, where A(v)=0 for v<=0 and A(v)=A(g(v))+g(v-g(v)-1)+1 for v>=1. Equivalently, v is a term iff A(v)-A(v-1)=1.
%e A395718 For v=9, m=floor(9/phi)=5 and L=3. The interval {6,7,8} contains the two previously admitted numbers 6 and 7, so c=2. Since 2*phi>3, the number 9 is excluded.
%e A395718 For v=10, m=floor(10/phi)=6 and L=3. The interval {7,8,9} contains only the previously admitted number 7, so c=1. Since phi<=3, the number 10 is admitted.
%p A395718 g := n -> iquo(isqrt(5*n^2)-n, 2):
%p A395718 A := proc(n) option remember; local m; if n <= 0 then return 0 end if; m := g(n); return A(m)+g(n-m-1)+1 end proc:
%p A395718 select(n -> A(n)-A(n-1)=1, [seq(n, n=1..200)]);
%t A395718 g[n_Integer?NonNegative] := Quotient[Floor[Sqrt[5 n^2]] - n, 2]
%t A395718 A[n_Integer] /; n <= 0 := 0
%t A395718 A[n_Integer?Positive] := A[n] = A[g[n]] + g[n - g[n] - 1] + 1
%t A395718 Select[Range[200], A[#] - A[# - 1] == 1 &]
%o A395718 (Python)
%o A395718 from functools import cache
%o A395718 from math import isqrt
%o A395718 def g(n):
%o A395718     return (isqrt(5*n*n)-n)//2
%o A395718 @cache
%o A395718 def A(n):
%o A395718     if n <= 0:
%o A395718         return 0
%o A395718     m = g(n)
%o A395718     return A(m)+g(n-m-1)+1
%o A395718 print(*(n for n in range(1, 201) if A(n)-A(n-1) == 1), sep=", ")
%Y A395718 Cf. A001622 (golden ratio), A000045 (Fibonacci numbers), A000032 (Lucas numbers), A003849 (Fibonacci word), A005206 (Hofstadter G-sequence, with A005206(n)=g(n+1)), A095076 (a Zeckendorf-related morphic sequence), A099267 (a different sequence also called the golden sieve), and A285520 (positions of 1 in a related morphic word having density 1/phi).
